z0a|v|To grow or become white; as, his cheek blanched with fear; the rose blanches in the sun. ; To avoid, as from fear; to evade; to leave unnoticed. ; To cause to turn aside or back; as, to blanch a deer. ; To use evasion.	o:aadc,79,1~aadc,79,1	s:70j5,20,3~70j5,20,3	d:axy,6,5;4wan,5,6~4wan,5,6;axy,6,5
z0a|a|To take the color out of, and make white; to bleach; as, to blanch linen; age has blanched his hair. ; To bleach by excluding the light, as the stalks or leaves of plants, by earthing them up or tying them together. ; To make white by removing the skin of, as by scalding; as, to blanch almonds. ; To whiten, as the surface of meat, by plunging into boiling water and afterwards into cold, so as to harden the surface and retain the juices. ; To give a white luster to (silver, before stamping, in the process of coining.). ; To cover (sheet iron) with a coating of tin. ; Fig.: To whiten; to give a favorable appearance to; to whitewash; to palliate.	j:aadc,79,1~aadc,79,1
